title: Hanoi's Train Street in 2026 — the whole story
format: story
charstyle: nutshell
voice: af_heart
music: upbeat
score: upbeat@1 cafe@2 emotional@6 upbeat@8 emotional@7 upbeat@10
sources: Wikipedia "Hanoi Train Street" (1902 French line; residents since 1956; Oct 2019 closure; reopened 2023); seasia.co 22 Jul 2026 (alley ~4 m wall to wall, ~1.5 m rail-to-wall; barricades Sept 2022); VietNamNet 11 Aug 2026 (7 Aug 2026 a train knocked a tourist's phone at Nhà Dầu, Văn Miếu ward; café owner cited for selling in the railway safety corridor; legal corridor 8.6 m each side, homes 3–4 m from the track); Hanoi Times / Hanoi Tourism Dept (Mar 2025: agencies asked to stop tours; notice 6 Feb 2025); Znews/Mekong ASEAN 6 Jan 2026 (Hanoi asks to end passenger trains Hà Nội–Gia Lâm; cafés since 2018); CAND 5 Feb 2026 (Hải Phòng trains start/end at Gia Lâm, Lào Cai trains via Văn Điển, Long Biên bridge repairs, no end date); VnEconomy 28 Feb 2026 & VnExpress 4 Mar 2026 (freight to stop, passenger trains reduced, target before Jul 2026). Checked Oct 2026.
// ANGLE   The most filmed street in Hanoi is illegal to sit on — and in 2026 its famous stretch lost its passenger trains.
// HERO    Dave (our backpacker) wants THE Train Street moment. BUT the law says no; THEREFORE crackdowns; BUT the stools come back;
//         BUT a train takes a phone; THEREFORE 2026 changes the line — Dave waits for a train that never comes; THEREFORE better ways.
// LOOK    one illustrated set (side view across the tracks), a cast that acts, real train, wind, foam, lanterns. Info lives IN the scene.
// COLOUR  sunset gold → sepia past → daylight rule → night quiet → sunset bridge → bright carriage → Ha Giang dusk.
// SOUND   crossing bell before every train, horn, crowd laugh on the foam moustache, glass crack on the phone, clock ticks in the wait.
// VIRAL   "this is Dave" cold open + a train in 6 s; a twist (no trains); a save card acted out; open loop to Ha Giang.

// YOUTUBE CUT (16:9, ~3:30): one continuous story with Dave — no chapters. Re-hooks: the stool is illegal (0:50), the phone (1:40), the twist (2:10).
# vis kind=ts act=hook bellCap=1 trainCap=2 head="1.5 METRES|between a train and your coffee"
This is Dave. Dave is drinking egg coffee… on a railway.
That bell means one thing.
Move.
One and a half metres. That's how close a train gets on Hanoi's Train Street.
And in 2026, whether you can still sit here is a stranger story than yes or no.
# vis kind=ts act=history trans=match tc=.5,.55 tc2=.5,.6
It started in 1902, when the French laid this line through the city.
From 1956, railway families lived right beside it, laundry over the tracks.
Around 2018, someone put out a coffee table, and the photos went everywhere.
And then… everyone came.
# vis kind=ts act=hook time=night bellCap=1 trainCap=2 measure=0 trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.5 head="THE RITUAL|sit · order · pull your knees in"
By night it works like a ritual. You sit on a plastic stool and order a coffee or a beer.
A bell rings. Everyone stands and steps back to the wall.
Then a full-size train fills the alley.
# vis kind=ts act=rule trans=whip head="8.6 METRES|the railway safety corridor"
But there's a problem nobody reads on the chalkboard.
By law, a railway keeps eight point six metres clear on each side.
These homes are three to four metres from the track.
So every stool on this street sits inside the safety corridor.
# vis kind=ts act=crack trans=slices
October 2019: the city banned visitors.
September 2022: barricades at the entrances.
March 2025: Hanoi's tourism department asked agencies to stop running tours here.
And every time, the stools came right back.
# vis kind=ts act=phone trans=whip head="AUGUST 2026|a train took her phone"
Then, on the seventh of August 2026, a tourist leaned in to film a passing train.
The train clipped the phone right out of her hand.
She walked away unhurt. The café owner was cited for selling inside the corridor.
# vis kind=ts act=quiet trans=bloom tc=.5,.3 tcol=#ffd98a head="2026|the twist"
But the biggest change in 2026 wasn't a ban. It was the trains.
In February, while Long Bien bridge was repaired, Hai Phong trains moved to Gia Lam, and Lao Cai trains went around the city.
So on the famous northern stretch, by Phung Hung, Dave can wait… and wait.
Hanoi now wants passenger trains off this line for good, and freight is set to stop too.
The trains you can still see run on the south side, past Le Duan and Kham Thien.
# vis kind=ts act=bridge trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.4 head="LONG BIEN|at sunset"
So if you want the feeling without the ticket, walk Long Bien bridge at sunset, the river turning gold under the old iron.
# vis kind=ts act=window trans=whip head="OR RIDE IT|wave from the inside"
Or take a train south from Hanoi station, and watch the alleys slide past the window.
Same street, from the inside. Nobody has to pull their knees in.
# vis kind=ts act=rules trans=blobs head="SAVE THIS"
And if you still go: stay off the rails.
No seats by the track.
Stay behind the barrier and listen to the officers.
And check that trains still run that day.
# vis kind=ts act=tease trans=zoomthru tc=.5,.5 head="NEXT|the Ha Giang Loop"
Train Street was never really about the train. It was about how close Hanoi lets you get.
Next time, Dave rides the Ha Giang Loop, and finds out what four days really cost.
